{
  "page": 62,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "330",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        39.52751922607422,
        57.665863037109375,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein",
      "bbox": [
        78.57550048828125,
        39.52751922607422,
        316.3913879394531,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(a) From our implementations, ideal use cases consist of highly non-linear dynamic equations for a",
      "bbox": [
        53.86858367919922,
        57.378700256347656,
        438.23040771484375,
        65.6905517578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "small dimension dx of the state space, large dimension dθ of the static parameter and potentially",
      "bbox": [
        69.11146545410156,
        67.34122467041016,
        438.2254638671875,
        76.99810791015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "large length T of the time series. In our cases dx was 2 or 3, dθ up to 20 and T between 100 and 400.",
      "bbox": [
        69.110107421875,
        77.3038101196289,
        438.2233581542969,
        86.960693359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(b) In particle Metropolis–Hastings (PMH) sampling, non-adaptive MCMC proposals for θ (e.g. tuned",
      "bbox": [
        53.86767578125,
        86.5132827758789,
        438.2222900390625,
        95.57830810546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "according to presimulation chains or burn-in iterations) would be costly for large T and require",
      "bbox": [
        69.11053466796875,
        97.22904205322266,
        438.22222900390625,
        105.5408935546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "that N is kept ﬁxed over the whole run of the Markov chain. Adaptive MCMC proposals such",
      "bbox": [
        69.11050415039062,
        107.1916275024414,
        438.2266540527344,
        115.50347900390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "as the adaptive Metropolis sampler (Roberts and Rosenthal, 2009) avoid such issues and proved",
      "bbox": [
        69.11050415039062,
        117.15421295166016,
        438.22943115234375,
        125.466064453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "particularly relevant for large dθ and T.",
      "bbox": [
        69.11050415039062,
        127.1167984008789,
        217.41310119628906,
        136.77386474609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(c) For intractable joint likelihood pθ.y1:T |x1:T /, we could design a sequential Monte Carlo (SMC)–",
      "bbox": [
        53.86775207519531,
        136.32644653320312,
        438.22491455078125,
        146.63787841796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "approximate Bayesian computation algorithm (see for example Peters et al. (2010) and Ratmann",
      "bbox": [
        69.11041259765625,
        147.04222106933594,
        438.2239990234375,
        155.35406494140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(2010), chapter 1) for a ﬁxed approximate Bayesian computation tolerance \", using the approxima-",
      "bbox": [
        69.111328125,
        157.0048065185547,
        438.2240295410156,
        165.316650390625
      ],
      "direction": [
